{
  "requirement": "A node's structural hash MUST be the SHA-256 digest of an unambiguous encoding of\nits outline-relevant structure, including siblings its profile attaches to it:\nthe kinds of its named grammar nodes and, in source order, its text pieces. A\ntext piece MUST be the text of a leaf token, or text inside a node that no child\ntoken covers. Each text piece MUST be hashed as its ordered list of words, where\nwords are separated by whitespace, the whitespace itself, including leading and\ntrailing whitespace, is never hashed, and each word is encoded separately, so\n`ab cd` and `abcd` differ. The same rule MUST apply in every language. Comments\nMUST be excluded, except comment kinds the profile includes; the Rust profile MUST include doc comments. For an included comment, only its text and its\nprofile's comment group MUST be hashed, not its delimiter tokens such as `//`,\n`///`, `//!`, `/*`, `*/`, or a leading `*`; the Rust profile's groups are inner\nand outer doc comments. The words of consecutive included comments of one group\nMUST be hashed as one ordered list, so reflowing them across more or fewer lines\nor comments does not change the hash, while moving text between groups does. A `line`\nnode's hash MUST be computed by the same word rule over the line's text. A node's\nidentity hash MUST be computed the same way but MUST also exclude its outline\ndescendants and their attached siblings, so editing a descendant does not change\nits ancestors' selectors.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check every obligation of `requirements.references.structural_hash`. No obligation is left untested, and no acknowledgments apply. I read only the test sources supplied, plus a search of the outline implementation to see which profiles attach siblings.\n\n- **SHA-256 over an unambiguous encoding, with kinds of named grammar nodes.**\n  - `hashes_include_grammar_kinds_nesting_and_sha256_digests` checks the standard SHA-256 empty vector.\n  - It checks a known digest of a tagged, length-prefixed text token for the `hello` line.\n  - Identical text `x;` parsed under the Rust and Python grammars gives different hashes, so kinds are hashed.\n  - A nested encoding differs from a sibling encoding, so the encoding is unambiguous.\n- **Text pieces, words and whitespace.**\n  - `prose_words_are_encoded_separately` and `reflowing_markdown_paragraphs_keeps_the_hash` check that `ab cd` differs from `abcd` and that reflowed whitespace is not hashed.\n  - `markdown_hashes_include_paragraph_text_between_punctuation_tokens` covers text inside a node that no child token covers, and checks that whitespace is not hashed.\n  - `string_literals_hash_as_words` covers string literals in Rust and TOML, and block scalars in YAML.\n  - `plain_text_line_hashes_ignore_whitespace_but_not_word_breaks` covers leading, trailing and internal whitespace, and the `line` node rule.\n- **Same rule in every language.** The language-specific tests above and `comments_are_excluded_from_hashes_in_every_other_profile` exercise Rust, Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, YAML and plain text.\n- **Comments excluded, except the profile's included kinds.**\n  - `comments_are_excluded_from_hashes_in_every_other_profile` checks that comment edits and removals leave the hash unchanged in each non-Rust profile. In each, a changed non-comment word changes it.\n  - `hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments` checks plain Rust comments are excluded and doc comments and attributes are included.\n- **Included comments hash only their text and group.**\n  - `reflowing_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash` covers `///` against `/** * */`, extra whitespace, blank doc lines, and reordered or changed words.\n  - `reflowing_inner_and_block_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash` covers `//!` against `/*! */` and `/** * */` against `///`.\n  - `editing_a_doc_comment_word_changes_the_hash` checks that changed words and word breaks change the hash while reflow does not.\n  - `inner_and_outer_doc_comments_hash_in_separate_groups` checks that moving text between the inner and outer groups changes the hash and that splitting words within a group does not.\n- **Attached siblings.** Only the Rust and Python profiles attach siblings. `identity_hashes_include_own_attached_siblings_but_not_descendants` covers Rust attributes and outer doc comments. `python_decorators_attach_to_the_decorated_definition_for_hashing` covers Python decorators.\n- **Identity hash.**\n  - `identity_hashes_include_own_attached_siblings_but_not_descendants`, `hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments` and `python_decorators_attach_to_the_decorated_definition_for_hashing` check that editing a descendant, including its attached doc comment or attribute, changes the ancestor's structural hash but not its identity hash.\n  - `anonymous_selectors_use_identity_hashes` and `anonymous_identities_survive_reflow_and_sibling_edits_and_number_duplicates` check that anonymous selectors, which depend on the identity hash, survive reflow and edits to descendants and siblings.\n- **End-to-end.** `outline_inputs_survive_moves_and_unhashed_comments_but_not_edits` checks that moves and unhashed comment edits keep a recorded hash valid and that real edits make it stale. `hashes_do_not_depend_on_credential_values` covers a redaction interaction.\n\nAll of these use real assertions on hash equality or inequality, and the expected results match the requirement text.",
  "references": "43cc578b39e90b01d883677c61f178ab831120da3e5a9ccfbc4c9fed500d1f06",
